1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,741
Two hall tickets.

2
00:00:01,741 --> 00:00:06,048
One says seat three comma five,
the other says five comma three.

3
00:00:06,048 --> 00:00:07,339
Same two numbers.

4
00:00:07,339 --> 00:00:08,239
Same seat?

5
00:00:08,617 --> 00:00:09,517
No.

6
00:00:09,034 --> 00:00:10,850
They are two different desks.

7
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:14,073
Swap the numbers, and you
sit in someone else's seat.

8
00:00:14,523 --> 00:00:17,231
Aarav learned this on his first exam day.

9
00:00:17,581 --> 00:00:20,167
So when does the order
of two numbers matter?

10
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:24,825
The answer is the ordered pair, the
building block of this whole chapter.

11
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:29,842
Last time, every element of A cross
B was a pair in round brackets.

12
00:00:29,842 --> 00:00:32,074
Let us look closely at that pair.

13
00:00:32,474 --> 00:00:36,781
An ordered pair is two objects in a
fixed order, inside round brackets,

14
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:38,521
with a comma between them.

15
00:00:38,921 --> 00:00:41,379
The one on the left is the first element.

16
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:43,923
The one on the right
is the second element.

17
00:00:44,323 --> 00:00:47,609
For a seat in the hall, the
first number is the column.

18
00:00:47,609 --> 00:00:49,382
The second number is the row.

19
00:00:50,032 --> 00:00:51,594
Watch the seat finder.

20
00:00:51,594 --> 00:00:56,037
For three comma five, walk three
columns across, then five rows up.

21
00:00:56,437 --> 00:01:00,797
For five comma three, walk five
columns across, then three rows up.

22
00:01:00,797 --> 00:01:02,031
A different desk.

23
00:01:02,431 --> 00:01:06,418
Now keep the second number at three,
and let the first number, a, count

24
00:01:06,418 --> 00:01:07,318
up.

25
00:01:07,205 --> 00:01:09,207
The blue dot is a comma three.

26
00:01:09,207 --> 00:01:12,197
Its twin, three comma
a, is the swapped pair.

27
00:01:12,597 --> 00:01:16,021
They land on the same desk
only once, when a is three.

28
00:01:16,021 --> 00:01:19,417
Swapping changes the seat,
unless both numbers are equal.

29
00:01:20,067 --> 00:01:21,093
Side by side.

30
00:01:21,093 --> 00:01:24,514
Curly brackets make a set,
and order does not matter.

31
00:01:24,514 --> 00:01:27,688
The set three, five equals
the set five, three.

32
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:31,983
Round brackets make an ordered
pair, and order is everything.

33
00:01:31,983 --> 00:01:34,690
Three comma five is not five comma three.

34
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:36,307
Now you try.

35
00:01:36,307 --> 00:01:40,839
Is two comma two a proper ordered
pair, even though both entries are the

36
00:01:40,839 --> 00:01:41,739
same?

37
00:01:41,392 --> 00:01:42,606
Pause and think.

38
00:01:45,806 --> 00:01:46,706
Yes!

39
00:01:46,305 --> 00:01:49,035
It is the desk in column two, row two.

40
00:01:49,035 --> 00:01:50,952
A pair may repeat an entry.

41
00:01:51,602 --> 00:01:53,659
So when are two ordered pairs equal?

42
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:58,941
In NCERT words: two ordered pairs are
equal if and only if the corresponding

43
00:01:58,941 --> 00:02:03,327
first elements are equal, and the
second elements are also equal.

44
00:02:03,727 --> 00:02:08,809
In symbols: a comma b equals c comma
d, exactly when a equals c, and b

45
00:02:08,809 --> 00:02:09,709
equals d.

46
00:02:10,426 --> 00:02:11,888
Both checks must pass.

47
00:02:11,888 --> 00:02:16,007
Three comma five and three comma four
share the first element, but they

48
00:02:16,007 --> 00:02:17,321
are different pairs.

49
00:02:18,071 --> 00:02:19,040
Remember this.

50
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:21,547
In an ordered pair, position is meaning.

51
00:02:21,547 --> 00:02:24,208
Swap the entries, and you get a new pair.

52
00:02:24,908 --> 00:02:26,235
Now a big result.

53
00:02:26,235 --> 00:02:29,068
Is A cross B the same set as B cross A?

54
00:02:29,418 --> 00:02:30,318
Step one.

55
00:02:30,197 --> 00:02:33,495
Take A as one and two,
and take B as just three.

56
00:02:34,245 --> 00:02:35,145
Step two.

57
00:02:35,047 --> 00:02:38,480
A cross B is one comma
three, and two comma three.

58
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,592
The first elements come from A.

59
00:02:41,342 --> 00:02:42,242
Step three.

60
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:45,672
B cross A is three comma
one, and three comma two.

61
00:02:45,672 --> 00:02:48,037
Now the first elements come from B.

62
00:02:48,787 --> 00:02:49,687
Step four.

63
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:52,162
One comma three is not three comma one.

64
00:02:52,162 --> 00:02:55,585
So the two sets differ,
though each has two elements.

65
00:02:56,335 --> 00:03:01,419
So A cross B and B cross A differ,
unless A equals B, or one set is empty.

66
00:03:02,069 --> 00:03:03,186
Pairs can grow.

67
00:03:03,186 --> 00:03:06,596
Three objects in a fixed
order make an ordered triplet.

68
00:03:06,996 --> 00:03:10,863
A cross A cross A holds every
triplet with entries from A.

69
00:03:10,863 --> 00:03:15,699
If A has two elements, that is two
times two times two, eight triplets.

70
00:03:16,099 --> 00:03:20,712
And R cross R, pairs of real numbers,
is every point on a graph sheet.

71
00:03:20,712 --> 00:03:23,555
Each point is one ordered pair, x comma y.

72
00:03:24,205 --> 00:03:25,941
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:26,291 --> 00:03:31,683
If two x minus one comma y plus three
equals five comma one, find x and

74
00:03:31,683 --> 00:03:32,583
y.

75
00:03:32,657 --> 00:03:33,572
Step one.

76
00:03:33,572 --> 00:03:36,373
Equal pairs have equal first elements.

77
00:03:36,373 --> 00:03:38,616
Two x minus one equals five.

78
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:40,266
Step two.

79
00:03:40,139 --> 00:03:41,637
Add one, then halve.

80
00:03:41,637 --> 00:03:42,749
x equals three.

81
00:03:43,499 --> 00:03:44,399
Step three.

82
00:03:44,385 --> 00:03:46,610
The second elements are equal too.

83
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:48,296
y plus three equals one.

84
00:03:49,046 --> 00:03:49,946
Step four.

85
00:03:49,923 --> 00:03:51,162
Take away three.

86
00:03:51,162 --> 00:03:52,613
y equals minus two.

87
00:03:53,363 --> 00:03:54,263
Check.

88
00:03:53,987 --> 00:03:56,720
Two times three, minus one, is five.

89
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,777
Minus two plus three is one.

90
00:03:58,777 --> 00:03:59,718
Both match.

91
00:04:00,368 --> 00:04:01,834
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:02,184 --> 00:04:07,087
If x squared comma y plus one equals
nine comma four, many students write

93
00:04:07,087 --> 00:04:09,094
x equals three, and stop.

94
00:04:09,494 --> 00:04:11,935
But minus three squared is also nine.

95
00:04:11,935 --> 00:04:15,092
So x is three or minus
three, and y is three.

96
00:04:15,092 --> 00:04:19,014
Two answers: three comma three,
and minus three comma three.

97
00:04:19,664 --> 00:04:21,292
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:21,292 --> 00:04:25,062
In PUC exams: equal ordered
pairs, find x and y.

99
00:04:25,062 --> 00:04:29,039
In JEE Main, inside questions
that count ordered pairs.

100
00:04:29,639 --> 00:04:30,806
Exam level now.

101
00:04:30,806 --> 00:04:34,435
A is the set of integers
whose square is at most four.

102
00:04:34,435 --> 00:04:38,995
How many ordered pairs a comma b in
A cross A are different from b comma

103
00:04:38,995 --> 00:04:39,895
a?

104
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:40,617
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:46,017 --> 00:04:47,233
The answer is B.

106
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:48,133
Twenty.

107
00:04:48,356 --> 00:04:51,559
A is minus two, minus
one, zero, one and two.

108
00:04:51,559 --> 00:04:53,057
That is five integers.

109
00:04:53,557 --> 00:04:58,090
So A cross A has five times five,
which is twenty five ordered pairs.

110
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:01,697
A pair equals its swap
only when a equals b.

111
00:05:01,697 --> 00:05:03,163
That is five pairs.

112
00:05:03,663 --> 00:05:07,981
Twenty five minus five leaves twenty
pairs that change when swapped.

113
00:05:08,581 --> 00:05:10,042
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:10,042 --> 00:05:11,544
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:14,744 --> 00:05:17,742
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:18,142 --> 00:05:19,042
One.

117
00:05:18,745 --> 00:05:23,950
If x plus two comma four equals
five comma two y, find x and y.

118
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:25,750
Two.

119
00:05:25,341 --> 00:05:28,626
Is the set one, two equal
to the set two, one?

120
00:05:28,626 --> 00:05:31,314
Is one comma two equal to two comma one?

121
00:05:32,214 --> 00:05:33,114
Three.

122
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:37,672
If A is p and q, and B is r, are
A cross B and B cross A equal?

123
00:05:38,772 --> 00:05:42,170
Next time: Diya links classmates
to their blood groups.

124
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:46,501
Only some pairs are true, and that
chosen set of pairs is a relation.
